In article <C4IIqK.JpK@cs.uiuc.edu> droberts@cs.uiuc.edu (Donald B Roberts) writes:
>IS the dev team planning on supporting the Borland C++ compiler for OS/2? 

Well, being the person who "unofficially" supported CSet/2 under OS/2 and
an owner of BCC, I thought I'd give it a whack.  It turned into more
hassle than I had time for...as I will explain below.

One problem is that Borland's make will not allow macro substitutions
like $* within EXPLICIT rules.  This will cause some problems with the
given Nethack Makefile for OS/2.  Not too bad, but it would be a fair amount
of editing to get around.  I opted for ftp'ing a version of dmake
or sticking with nmake.  Either one will work.  But...

>Actually I would simply like some hints on converting the source code over.
>It is slightly difficult since there are no #defines pertinent to BCC and also
>since BCC defines the symbol __TURBOC__, I think some contradictory code is 
>being generated. (It thinks that its on an MS-DOS machine sometimes). Any
>help will be much appreciated.

This is one of the problems, but easily circumvented.  Whenever I found
an offending __TURBOC__ dependancy, I added 

#if (defined(__TURBOC__) && !defined(OS2_BCC))

etc...

The real problems come in because BCC uses two different style calling
conventions for the library functions (also labelled linkage specifier
but not to be confused with the C++ 'extern "C"' linkage specifier).
Basically, most of the library functions are __stdcall, but some
are __cdecl.  The difference between the two, according to the 
documentation is that a __stdcall routine itself is responsible for cleaning
up the stack when finished, while an __cdecl routine has the caller
clean up the stack.  If you don't have the proper prototype, including
calling convention, you will most likely trap.  The real pain in the
butt here is the fact that the library uses BOTH methods AND the fact
that variable argument routines like printf() CAN'T use the __stdcall
(because only the caller of printf() knows EXACTLY how many arguments
to pull off the stack).  

So, you must make sure that 

	A) all variable argument functions are __cdecl or you compile
with the default as __cdecl.  I opted for compiling __cdecl.

	B) all your prototypes for library routines come from the headers 
(due to the mixing of __cdecl and __stdcall) rather than the nice macros
provided within the Nethack code.  This will probably take some time,
but I don't have that now.

So, to summarize, it's not just the makefile that needs some work *and*
I don't have the time to go through all the code right now and check for
the header declarations...

